# Project Brief

## GIS-Based Spatial Assessment of Elements in Escravos River Sediments

### Project Overview

For this project, I will use GIS to examine how the concentrations of six elements vary across sediment sampling locations along the Escravos River in Delta State, Nigeria.

The data come from an existing sediment study in which nine bottom-sediment samples were collected along approximately 28.5 km of the river and analysed using ICP-MS. The six elements measured are Thorium (Th), Uranium (U), Niobium (Nb), Rubidium (Rb), Barium (Ba), and Zirconium (Zr).

I will use QGIS to map the nine sampling locations and compare the measured concentrations between them. This will make it easier to see where relatively higher and lower concentrations occur along the river.

### Spatial Question

How do Th, U, Nb, Rb, Ba, and Zr concentrations vary spatially along the Escravos River, and which sampling locations show relatively higher concentrations that may warrant further environmental monitoring?

### Study Area

The project covers an approximately 28.5 km stretch of the Escravos River in Delta State, Nigeria, extending from the Jones Creek junction to Areton, before the Gulf of Guinea.

The dataset contains nine sediment sampling locations along this section of the river, including Jones' Creek Tributary, Okerenkoko, Kurutie, Areton, Escravos Water Front, Benin River Junction and Benikrukru Entrance.

